      "writtenOn": "2025-04-24T19:48:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When we call `prepareFlush()` because we ran out of room to fit the next reservation, `DBM-\u003egetVertexWriter()` has already moved off of the buffer that needs to be zero\u0027ed, so `padAndZero()` calling `getVertexWriter()` again will be getting a byte range in the next buffer.\n\nBut at this point, `Appender::fNextWriter` should still have its offset starting at the spot in the old buffer that needs to be zero\u0027ed.  Or is this the issue around fPendingCOunt not being correct all the time that you mentioned offline?\n\nLike if we mostly filled up a buffer, then called flush() externally because of astate change, and then did another reservation that had to move to a new buffer right away. In this scenario `fPendingCount \u003d\u003d 0` because of the state change flush.\n\nI think we can still reconstruct the required bytes needing to be zero\u0027ed by looking at `fTarget.fOffset + fTarget.fSize`. If we round this ending byte offset up to a multiple of 4*stride, then the difference is the number of bytes to zero. `fNextWriter` should be at the right spot to start zero\u0027ing and should have a mark that is far enough out (it\u0027d be the pessimistic reservation that includes any returned bytes) we can call `zeroBytes(...)` on it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 403,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 403,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "bc9688513c1fceae4b2af1e6889302b5bac1b2ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "99e517db_a96b94d4",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawWriter.h",
        "patchSetId": 23
      },
      "lineNbr": 403,
      "author": {
        "id": 3948337
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-04-24T21:34:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good catch, I did not think of this interaction when changing the pad/zeroing to `getVertexWriter()`. So this definitely needs to get fixed.\n\nThe issue with `fPendingCount` is that if `fPendingCount \u003d\u003d 0` and then you call a \"naked reserve call\" followed by an append\u0027s reserve call, both offsets get count 4 aligned, because the naked reserve does not increment `fPendingCount`, but the buffer offset does get moved forward.\n\nThe other scenario, which I believe is the same as you are describing, is a \"naked reserve\" followed by an append large enough to trigger a new buffer, where\nagain,`fPendingCount` says one thing but the buffer offset is somewhere else.\n\nHOWEVER, after going through it again, I realized there is a deeper problem here: simply asking for an aligned count reservation does not always guarantee that there is an aligned count amount of room in the buffer and writer because of the interaction with returning vertex bytes. Using `getVertexWriter` to do the padding/zeroing masked this interaction because that writer would always have enough room.\n\nI\u0027ll keep hacking at it!",
      "parentUuid": "f7922ac8_72856e92",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 403,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 403,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "bc9688513c1fceae4b2af1e6889302b5bac1b2ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6cf3aba0_a4e5e75c",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawWriter.h",
        "patchSetId": 23
      },
      "lineNbr": 403,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-04-25T01:47:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For the first situation, the append\u0027s reserve call shouldn\u0027t be doing anything to change the buffer offset. It should end up using the space that was already reserved from the first call. And if it didn\u0027t fit, then the first reserve\u0027s entire reservation would have returned like it never happened, right? What causes the buffer offset to move in the naked reserve call?\n\nIn the second situation, you\u0027re right that the fPendingCount could be misleading as it reflects the post-flush state. My idea to use `fTarget` also doesn\u0027t work as-is because the flush resets fTarget. Although this is only a problem if a state-change triggers a flush (pipeline-triggering flushes already pad and zero, and a \"ran-out-of-room\" flush hasn\u0027t modified fPendingCount or fTarget yet). \n\nPerhaps if flush() resets fTarget\u0027s fOffset to be `fOffset + fPendingCount*stride` to position (and reduce fSize to appropriately), the appending target still defines whatever space was left in the original reservation that had been returned by `getVertexWriter()` inside reserve(). \n\n\nIn the 3rd situation, I\u0027m not following how the returning of bytes prevents us from not having enough bytes to be able zero out to a 4-aligned count. I can see how calling `getVertexWriter` for the bytes to zero could accidentally go to the next buffer if we hadn\u0027t returned bytes yet (although when padding-and-zero\u0027ing, we should have already returned bytes or shouldn\u0027t have an active appender, so this might mean that some other calculation was messing up how many bytes we were trying to return?).\n\nAnd I wouldn\u0027t expect to always have room for 4*stride space at the end, but there should be enough to fill up to a multiple of 4*stride. I\u0027d need to see a step-by-step breakdown of what\u0027s happening to see where it\u0027s breaking down.",
      "parentUuid": "99e517db_a96b94d4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 403,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 403,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "bc9688513c1fceae4b2af1e6889302b5bac1b2ad",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
